/// Chapter 16 of the guide. This page is its source: edit it here.
page Learn16Testing(path: "/learn/16-testing", title: "Testing", description: "How to write wf tests: render tests with snapshots, seeded stores, tests that click and type in headless Chrome, wf verify, and running them in CI.", layout: DocsShell("Testing", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.build"), blurb: "Tests written in WebFluent: render a component and read what it shows, or click through it in a real browser — then check every page in one.", source: "site/src/pages/guide/16-testing.wf", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "render-tests", label: "Render tests"), Anchor(id: "interaction-tests", label: "Interaction tests"), Anchor(id: "seeding-data-and-stores", label: "Seeding data and stores"), Anchor(id: "page-tests", label: "Page tests"), Anchor(id: "fakes", label: "Fakes"), Anchor(id: "running-tests", label: "Running tests"), Anchor(id: "wf-verify", label: "wf verify"), Anchor(id: "ci", label: "CI"), Anchor(id: "what-to-test", label: "What to test"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Write a `test` that renders a component and holds it to `expect` and a snapshot", "Seed a test's values and stores with `data:`", "Click, type and press keys through a page with steps that run in headless Chrome", "Answer a service with a `fake`, and check what was called with `expect called`", "Load every built page with `wf verify`, and run it all in CI"], prevTo: "/learn/15-data", prevLabel: t("ch.15"), nextTo: "/learn/17-pdf-and-slides", nextLabel: t("ch.17"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter is about testing a WebFluent project: render a component and check what it shows, click and type through a page in a real browser, and load every page of the built site to catch errors before a reader does.\n\nYou write tests in WebFluent itself, as `test \"name\" \{ … \}` declarations. A test renders something — a component, a fragment of a page, a whole page — and holds it to what it must and must not show. A test that clicks or types runs in a headless browser. `wf verify` then loads every page of the built site in one.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("- A project with at least one component or page to test.\n- For a test that clicks, types or presses a key, and for `wf verify`: Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or the `WF_CHROME` environment variable pointing at one. A test that only looks needs neither.")

        Heading("Render tests", id: "render-tests").h2

        Markdown("To check what a component shows, place it in a test and say what the text must and must not contain. Put tests in `tests/*.wf`, or beside the code in `src/`. A test has the project's components, stores, types and constants at hand.\n\nThis file declares a price tag and three tests of it:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"component PriceTag(_ amount: Number, sale: Bool = false) {
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Text(format(amount, .currency)).bold
        if sale { Badge("Sale").danger }
    }
}

test "a price tag shows the amount as currency" {
    PriceTag(1234.5)
    expect "$1,234.50"
    expect not "Sale"
}

test "a sale tag carries the badge" {
    PriceTag(10, sale: true)
    expect "Sale"
}

test "a list renders each row"(data: { rows: [{ name: "Ada" }, { name: "Grace" }] }) {
    for r in rows { Text(r.name) }
    expect "Ada"
    expect "Grace"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Run `wf test`, and each test prints a line; all three pass. The parts of a test that looks:\n\n- `expect \"text\"` — the text must appear in what the page shows. `expect not \"text\"` — it must not.\n- `data: \{ … \}` — values the body reads, by name. A `state` in the body starts with its initial value.\n- `t(\"key\")` — reads the project's translations, in its default locale.")

        Heading("How expect reads text", id: "how-expect-reads-text").h3

        Markdown("`expect` reads the text a reader sees, never the markup: a class name in the HTML does not count. It reads the text laid out as a browser lays it out:\n\n- runs of white space are one space;\n- a block, or anything a `Row`, `Stack` or `Grid` lays out, is set apart from what is beside it;\n- inline elements that touch read as touching, so `Text \{ Code(\"live\") Code(\": \") Code(\"[Deploy]\") \}` reads `live: [Deploy]`.\n\nThe line breaks and indentation of the snapshot are not text. A render and a browser read the same page the same way.\n\nIn a browser, a page may still be moving — a row on its way out, a fetch on its way in. `expect` reads the page again until it holds, for up to a second and a half.")

        Heading("Snapshots", id: "snapshots").h3

        Markdown("Each render is also compared to a *snapshot*, the HTML saved in `tests/__snapshots__/<file>/<test>.html`. `wf test` writes a snapshot when it is missing and fails, with a diff, when a render no longer matches it. After a change you meant, run `wf test --update` to rewrite them.")

        Heading("Interaction tests", id: "interaction-tests").h2

        Markdown("To check what a click, a keystroke or typed text does, add steps that act. A click cannot be rendered — it runs a handler, which runs an action, which changes a store, which repaints — so a test with an action in it is compiled into a real page, served, and run in a headless browser. It uses the same compiler, the same runtime and the same output a reader gets.\n\nThis test types an incident, reports it, and checks the count goes up:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"store IncidentStore {
    state titles: [String] = ["Disk full", "Login slow"]
    derived open = titles.length
    action report(title: String) { titles = titles.concat([title]) }
}

test "reporting an incident opens one more" {
    use IncidentStore
    state draft = ""
    Text("{IncidentStore.open} open")
    Input(bind: draft, label: "What happened")
    Button("Report") { on click { IncidentStore.report(draft) } }

    expect "2 open"
    type "Queue backing up" into "What happened"
    click "Report"
    expect "3 open"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The steps run **in the order you write them**. That order is the test: what a click did is visible only in the `expect` that follows it.")

        Table(caption: "Write a test that acts") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Step").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`expect \"text\"` · `expect not \"text\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("What the page must, or must not, show")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`expect focus \"Beta\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("What has the focus is called that: its `aria-label`, its label, its placeholder or its text") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`click \"Save\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Clicks whatever carries that name: a button or a link, a control, a tab, a menu's item, an option, a row of a tree. The focus goes to it, as a real click puts it there")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`type \"Ada\" into \"Name\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Types into the control that label names")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` · `press \"Escape\" in \"Search\"` · `press \"mod+k\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A key, on the focused element or a named one, with the modifiers a combination names (`mod` is ⌘ on a Mac, Ctrl elsewhere); `Escape` closes an open `Modal` or `Dialog` as a real one would") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A step finds what it acts on the way a reader does: by the name on it. A button's name is its text; a control's is its label, its placeholder or its `aria-label`. The accessibility checks hold a page to the same names ([Accessibility](/about/accessibility)), so a test that passes here is a page somebody can use.\n\nIt finds only what a reader can reach. A button in a closed `Sheet`, a branch that is hidden, or anything under `inert` is passed over, so `click \"Juice\"` presses the one that shows, not its namesake in a sheet that is shut. When two a reader could mean carry the name, the step fails and says so, rather than guessing: name the one meant more closely (`aria-label: \"Remove Tea\"`). And `expect` reads what is drawn above the page as well: an open dialog, such as the one `ask(…)` draws, and the toasts.")

        DocsCallout("**Coming from Testing Library?** There is no `getByTestId` and no `waitFor`. A step finds an element only by the name a reader sees, so a control with no label cannot be clicked or typed into, and `expect` waits on its own, for up to a second and a half. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react).", kind: .note)

        DocsCallout("**A handler that throws fails the test**, even when every `expect` would have passed. A click that quietly did nothing is the bug, not a pass.", kind: .important)

        Markdown("A test that acts takes no snapshot: its expects are the test.")

        Heading("The test page", id: "the-test-page").h3

        Markdown("The page is drawn as the built site is:\n\n- it links the project's scripts under `src/`, so a click that calls one runs it;\n- it bundles the project's stylesheets under `src/` after the theme's rules and before the rules `style \{ \}` blocks compile to;\n- it links the stylesheets `meta.stylesheets` names;\n- it serves `public/` beside it.\n\nSo a rule that hides a word, or one that names a font or an image under `public/`, behaves the same in a test as on the site. A test that only looks renders without any of them.")

        Heading("Testing portals", id: "testing-portals").h3

        Markdown("A `Portal` draws its block into a node that `into:` names, and only the running page draws it. So a test whose body holds a portal — or places a component that holds one — is compiled into a page and run in the browser too, with no snapshot:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"test "a portal draws into the element a handle names" {
    Stack(ref: spot)
    Portal(into: spot) { Text("in") }
    expect "in"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Seeding data and stores", id: "seeding-data-and-stores").h2

        Markdown("To start a test from a known state, name it in `data:`. A key that is a value's name supplies that value; a key that is a store's name seeds that store's state. Seeding works the same in a test that only looks and in one that clicks.\n\nA seed is held to the state it seeds before the test runs: `data: \{ count: \"two\" \}` for a `state count = 0` is `T33`, naming the place (`count is \"two\", not a Number`; a store's seed names it, `Cart.items[0] is 1, not a CartItem`), and a store member the store does not have is `T05`.\n\nThis test fills the cart with two of one item and checks the summary:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type CartItem { id: String, qty: Number }

store Cart {
    state items: [CartItem] = []
    derived count = items.map(i => i.qty).sum()
}

component CartSummary {
    use Cart
    Text("{Cart.count} items")
}

test "the cart shows what is in it"(data: { Cart: { items: [{ id: "a", qty: 2 }] } }) {
    CartSummary
    expect "2 items"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Page tests", id: "page-tests").h2

        Markdown("To click through a page as a reader does, place the page in a test by its name, as you would a component. This works for a page whose route takes no parameter. Its state, its stores and its handlers all run:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"test "a task is added with Enter" {
    Tasks
    type "Walk the dog" into "New task"
    press "Enter" in "New task"
    expect "Walk the dog"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` in a form's text field submits the form, as a real key does. The `spa` and `static` projects that `wf init` writes carry tests like this under `tests/`; run `wf test` in a new project to see them pass.")

        Heading("Fakes", id: "fakes").h2

        Markdown("A page that reads from a service has nothing to show in a test until something answers it, and a test should not depend on a server being up. A `fake` answers a group's endpoints in its place. The compiler holds it to the group's contract, as it holds a call: every endpoint it answers is one the group has, every name it binds is one of that endpoint's parameters, and every answer is the result the endpoint declares.\n\nThis test lists the team from a fake, and checks that adding a person with the wrong address shows the server's field error:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Person { id: String, name: String, email: Email }
type NewPerson { name: String, email: Email }
type FieldError { field: String, message: String }
type ValidationProblem = Problem { errors: [FieldError] }

enum CreatePersonError {
    unprocessableContent(problem: ValidationProblem) on 422
    http(error: HttpError)
}

api Backend(base: "/api") { }

api Backend.people {
    get list() at "people" -> [Person]!
    post create(body: NewPerson) at "people" -> Person ! CreatePersonError
    invalidates [list]
}

store Team {
    state problem = ""
    action add(name: String, email: Email) {
        match await Backend.people.create(body: NewPerson(name: name, email: email)) {
            .ok(_) { problem = "" }
            .err(.unprocessableContent(p)) { problem = p.errors.first()?.message ?? "Check the form" }
            .err(.http(e)) { problem = e.message }
        }
    }
}

page People(path: "/", title: "People", description: "The team.") {
    use Team
    state email: Email = "ada@example.com"
    resource people = Backend.people.list()
    Heading("People").h1
    match people {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { for p in list by p.id { Text(p.name) } }
    }
    Input(bind: email, label: "Email").email
    Button("Add") { on click { Team.add("Grace", email) } }
    Text(Team.problem)
}

fake OneEngineer for Backend.people {
    list() {
        .ok([Person(id: "1", name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com")])
    }
    create(body) {
        if body.email.domain() == "example.com" { .ok(Person(id: "2", name: body.name, email: body.email)) } else { .err(.unprocessableContent(ValidationProblem(title: "Invalid", errors: [FieldError(field: "email", message: "Use your work address")]))) }
    }
}

test "lists the team"(fakes: [OneEngineer]) {
    People
    expect "Ada"
    expect not called Backend.people.create
}

test "shows the server's field error"(fakes: [OneEngineer]) {
    People
    type "grace@elsewhere.org" into "Email"
    click "Add"
    expect "Use your work address"
    expect called Backend.people.create
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How a fake answers:\n\n- **By name.** `fake OneEngineer for Backend.people` answers that group; `fake … for Backend` answers a service's own endpoints. Each answer names the endpoint, then the parameters it reads — any of them, by the names the endpoint gives them. A parameter the call left out is its default, or `null`.\n- **As the endpoint would.** An answer is the endpoint's result: `.ok(value)`, or `.err(error)` with a case of its error enum, or an `HttpError` such as `.status(500, null)`. An `if` or a `match` picks one from what the call sent.\n- **Without the network.** A test that names `fakes:` runs in a browser, where its page makes the calls. Nothing is sent: a call goes to the fake, and a write's `invalidates` asks its reads again, as after a real success. A call to an endpoint no fake answers fails the test and names the endpoint.\n- **Whole.** A paged endpoint is answered a page at a time, from the parameter the page goes in; a `streamed` one with the whole list, which arrives a line at a time.\n\nWhen the contract comes from an OpenAPI spec (`wf api`, or `api … from`), a fake can answer from the spec's own examples: every endpoint it does not answer itself answers `.ok(example)`, with the example of a success the spec gives, held to the endpoint's result as an answer written by hand is.")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Person { id: String, name: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") { }

api Backend.people {
    get listPeople at "people" -> [Person]!
    post createPerson(body: Person) at "people" -> Person!
}

fake Examples for Backend.people from examples

fake Refusing for Backend.people from examples {
    createPerson(body) { .err(.status(503, null)) }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Here `Backend.people` is written by hand for the sake of the example; with `wf api` it is the generated file, and the examples are the spec's.\n\n`expect called Backend.people.create` passes once the page has asked that endpoint; `expect not called` passes while it has not. Use them to check that a write went out, or that a form did not send what it should not.\n\nA fake that does not fit its contract is `N06`, with what is wrong: an endpoint the group does not have, a parameter it does not take, an answer of the wrong type. So is a test's `fakes:` naming no fake, two fakes answering one endpoint, and an `expect called` naming no endpoint.\n\nPut fakes beside the tests that use them, under `tests/`, or in a file of their own there (`tests/fakes.wf`): a test may name a fake any file under `tests/` declares, and one beside it wins over another file's. A fake in `src/` is checked like any declaration, and a build leaves it out.")

        Heading("Running tests", id: "running-tests").h2

        Markdown("To run every test under `tests/`, and any declared in `src/`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test")
            ])

        Markdown("To run one file's tests, in the project it belongs to:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test tests/cart.wf")
            ])

        Markdown("To rewrite the snapshots after a change you meant:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test --update")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf test` prints a line per test and exits non-zero when one fails, so it works as a CI step as it is. A build never includes tests.")

        Heading("wf verify", id: "wf-verify").h2

        Markdown("To catch what only a running page shows, build the site and load every page of it:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && wf verify")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf verify` serves the build and opens every route in headless Chrome. It fails on:\n\n- an uncaught exception or a `console.error`;\n- a request that failed, or an image that did not load;\n- a page that rendered no text.\n\nFor each route it prints the first contentful paint, the size and the requests. It also lists the built-ins no page drew — a fact about coverage, not a failure. Its options:\n\n- `--budget 400` fails a route that takes more than 400 ms to paint;\n- `--json` prints the report for a pipeline;\n- `--returning-visitor` visits every route again with storage holding what the previous build's pages kept;\n- `--fakes tests/fakes.wf` builds the site with the fakes that file declares answering its endpoints, and loads that — for a site whose API is elsewhere, or not running. It writes that build aside and removes it after; the project's own `build/` is not touched.\n\n[CLI](/reference/cli/verify) has the details.")

        Heading("CI", id: "ci").h2

        Markdown("To run the formatter, the build, the tests and `wf verify` on every push, add a workflow such as this one to `.github/workflows/check.yml`:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"name: Check
on: [push, pull_request]
jobs:
  check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash
      - run: echo "$HOME/.webfluent/bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
      - run: wf fmt --check
      - run: wf build
      - run: wf test
      - run: wf verify --budget 1000"#, title: "yaml")

        Markdown("GitHub's Ubuntu runners come with Chrome, so the tests that act and `wf verify` run there as they do on your machine.")

        Heading("What to test", id: "what-to-test").h2

        Markdown("The type checker and the accessibility lints already catch a large class of mistakes. Tests are for the behaviour they cannot see:\n\n- **Components** with more than one state: each branch a prop can take.\n- **Stores**, through what a component shows of them, seeded with `data:`.\n- **Flows** a reader depends on — signing in, adding to a cart, submitting a form — as tests that act.\n- **Every page**, with `wf verify`, on every build.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Markdown("**A step cannot find what it names.** The test fails and prints what the page showed instead:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"  FAIL  tests/cart.wf — the count follows what is added
        nothing to click called "Publish"
        the page showed:
        Nothing here"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Check that the name matches the button's text or the control's label, placeholder or `aria-label` exactly, and that an earlier step has drawn it.\n\n**A test that acts says there is no browser.** Install Chrome or Chromium, or set `WF_CHROME` to the path of one.\n\n**A snapshot test fails after a change you meant.** Run `wf test --update` and commit the new snapshots.")

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("A step finds what it acts on by its accessible name, so the mistakes that break a test are the ones the accessibility lints already report.\n\n**A field with no label.** `type \"…\" into \"…\"` names a control by its label, placeholder or `aria-label`, and this one has none:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"page Tasks(path: "/", title: "Tasks", description: "What to do.") {
    state draft = ""
    Heading("Tasks").h1
    Input(bind: draft)
    Text("Next: {draft}")
}"#, title: "wf", expect: "A03")

        Markdown("Give it `label: \"New task\"`, and the test types `into \"New task\"` ([A03](/errors/A03)).\n\n**A button with no text.** `click \"…\"` finds a button by its text, and an empty one has nothing to find:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"page Tasks(path: "/", title: "Tasks", description: "What to do.") {
    state count = 0
    Heading("Tasks").h1
    Text("{count} done")
    Button { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf", expect: "A05")

        Markdown("Write its text, `Button(\"Mark done\")`, or give it an `aria-label:` ([A05](/errors/A05)).\n\n**An icon button with no label.** An `IconButton` shows no text, so its `label:` is the only name a step can use:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"page Tasks(path: "/", title: "Tasks", description: "What to do.") {
    state count = 0
    Heading("Tasks").h1
    Text("{count} done")
    IconButton(icon: "plus") { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf", expect: "A02")

        Markdown("Add `label: \"Add one\"`, and click it with `click \"Add one\"` ([A02](/errors/A02)).\n\nGoing further: [Security](/about/security) covers what the compiler guarantees, what it cannot, and the decisions left to you: markup, secrets, storage, headers and a deployment checklist.")
    }
}
